#
# arch/os/windows — Windows process services.
#
# The counterpart of arch/os/linux and arch/os/macos, and the same three
# questions: how a program reaches the kernel without a C library under it,
# how it draws randomness, and where the system call instructions in a run of
# code are. On Windows the first answer is not an instruction at all — the
# system call numbers belong to ntdll and change with every build — so it is
# kernel32 and Winsock, which are the system's interface and not a C library.
#
# The fourth — arming those instructions in this process and standing behind
# the trap — has no entry here yet. Unlike on macOS nothing forbids it: ntdll's
# text can be made writable by the process that maps it, and a vectored
# exception handler sees the illegal instruction before anything else does.
# README.md in this directory says what it would take.
#
menu "Windows process services"
	depends on PLATFORM = "windows"

config OS_IO
	bool "the system calls, without a C library to make them through"
	default y
	help
	  The _sys_ calls the rest of this tree makes when there is no C
	  library to make them through — _sys_read, _sys_write, _sys_open,
	  _sys_close, _sys_readlink, _sys_access, _sys_execve, _sys_exit,
	  _sys_getpid, _sys_getppid, _sys_gettid, _sys_gettimeofday,
	  _sys_sendto, _sys_recvfrom, _sys_stat — each one call into kernel32,
	  ntdll or Winsock and none into msvcrt. The string helpers beside them
	  are shared with every other platform layer and live in
	  <arch/os/str.h>.

	  There is no _syscall6() here. The instruction exists, but the numbers
	  it takes are renumbered by every Windows build and are ntdll's to
	  know, so a program that made one would be right on one machine.

	  What Windows does not have is made up, and io.c says how at each
	  place: an int descriptor is the HANDLE's value (0, 1 and 2 the
	  standard handles), errors come back as a negative errno translated
	  from GetLastError(), paths are UTF-8 turned into the UTF-16 the wide
	  API takes, and execve() starts the program, waits for it and exits
	  with its status.

	  hpc's logger is the caller that matters: its open and its write go
	  through here whenever this symbol is set (hpc/log/write.c).

config OS_ENTROPY
	bool "the entropy call, without a C library to make it through"
	default y
	help
	  _sys_getrandom(), over BCryptGenRandom() with the system-preferred
	  generator — no algorithm handle to open, nothing to fail at startup.
	  The flags are accepted and ignored, as on macOS: the generator never
	  blocks and there is one of it. A request longer than a ULONG is
	  filled in pieces.

	  _entropy_is_source_fd() is always false: there is no file on Windows
	  a read() draws randomness from.

	  Beside it is the entropy seam, <arch/os/windows/entropy/hooks.h>, the
	  same macro as on the other platforms.

config OS_ENTROPY_HOOKS
	bool
	depends on OS_ENTROPY
	help
	  Whether the entropy seam is asked for. Left to a consumer to turn on,
	  because a seam nothing reads is a call to nothing.

config OS_ENTROPY_HOOKS_HEADER
	string
	depends on OS_ENTROPY_HOOKS
	help
	  The header <arch/os/windows/entropy/hooks.h> includes, written as it
	  would be inside the angle brackets. Whatever it does not define falls
	  back to the same nothing.

config OS_SCAN
	bool "the syscall instruction sites in a run of code"
	depends on ARCH = "x86_64" || ARCH = "arm64"
	default y
	help
	  scan_run(): given a run of machine code, say where in it the system
	  call instructions are and which number each one makes.

	  On Windows those are ntdll's stubs and nothing else's. On arm64 the
	  number is the svc's own immediate and is read off the word; armed is
	  udf with the same immediate, so the trap carries the number. On
	  x86-64 the instruction is Linux's `syscall` and the decoder is
	  Linux's (compiled again, not copied); what differs is that a current
	  stub sets eax three instructions before the site, behind a test and
	  a branch, and the lookback knows that shape.

	  It is pure: no system calls, no allocation, no state. Nothing in this
	  tree links it on Windows yet.

endmenu
